  "live_records": {
    "q-53189": {
      "dom_id": "step-q53189",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14232470/assets/ff93274daa7f179f86343e59b7cf277c0e70555fdfdd3cce113c3cba149b08ac.png)\n\nAn airplane $A$ is flying horizontally at a constant height of $800\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the airplane and a nearby radar station $R$ is decreasing at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second. What is the speed of the aircraft as a function of $\\theta ?$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14232470/assets/29d06d071e6983d3487426b08d5a5548a611134dcf116674d199f6d3de03f246.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the airplane's altitude, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the radar station and the airplane. The quantity $y=800$ meters does not change with time because the airplane stays at a constant altitude.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{800}{x} \\\\ x & =800\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(800\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-800{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-0.05$ (negative because the angle is decreasing). Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-800{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-800{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )(-0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the aircraft is $40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
