    "q-314883": {
      "dom_id": "step-q314883",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14232470/assets/7de8ecd3d178cf07377aef5ca96ce28f638700e6284d151e3578ba11ed87327a.png)\n\nEthan notices a drone descending. He estimates that the distance between his location and the point on the ground directly beneath the drone is $150\\,\\text{ft}.$ If the angle of elevation $\\theta $ between him and the drone decreases at a rate of $0.1\\,\\text{rad/s},$ at what rate is its altitude decreasing when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}?$ Assume that the rate of decreasing altitude is **positive** and do not round the answer.\n\nThe drone's altitude is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft/s}.$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14232470/assets/5024a642ac27698d848577165b08e3158d3248d50c064a79432fcad6e2df90d5.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone’s altitude, and $x$ is the distance between Ethan and the shadow. The quantity $x=150\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between Ethan and the shadow is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{150} \\\\ y & =150\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(150\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-0.1$ (negative because the angle is decreasing) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})(-0.1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150(4)(-0.1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is decreasing at a rate of $60\\,\\text{ft/s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
